---
YUME carries TCP, UDP and IP traffic through a server you control. Its client
(`yume`) and server (`yumed`) speak YTP/1, the YUME Transport Protocol.

Yume (夢) means dream in Japanese. The dream is an internet anyone can reach.
The design puts security first, then stealth, then usable speed.
[Why YUME exists](docs/WHY_YUME.md) tells the story behind the name.

YUME `0.3.0-dev1` builds from source on Linux x86-64 and is in active
development, with an independent security review planned.

- Website: <https://yume.fixcraft.jp>
- Source: <https://github.com/FixCraft-Inc/yume>
- Issues: <https://github.com/FixCraft-Inc/yume/issues>

## What it does

Applications reach `yume` through a local SOCKS5 listener (TCP CONNECT and UDP
ASSOCIATE), or the whole Linux machine routes through a managed TUN device.
By default, `yume` carries connections over one session to `yumed`, which
opens the destination sockets allowed by its policy. Configured TCP circuits
can instead leave through two or three servers in one operator's cluster.

The connection opens with TLS 1.3 and HTTP/2 using a captured Chrome profile.
The tunnel carries records in WebSocket frames. The front door serves ordinary
HTTP/2 requests from the cover site. A fresh admission proof opens a carrier,
where clients authenticate with composite Ed25519 and ML-DSA-87 identities.
Session keys combine
ML-KEM-1024, X25519, a pre-shared key and the TLS exporter, and application
data uses one-use AES-256-GCM record keys. ML-KEM-1024 and ML-DSA-87 are in
NIST's highest security category, and recorded traffic stays protected if a
quantum computer later breaks X25519
([threat model](docs/THREAT_MODEL.md#cryptography)).

In a direct session, `yumed` ends the tunnel, so it knows which client connected
and where its traffic goes. HTTPS inside the tunnel keeps page content between
the application and the site. [Implementation status](docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md)
lists what each part is tested with.

## Build

@code bash
git clone https://github.com/FixCraft-Inc/yume.git
cd yume
./ezbuild.sh
@end

This builds `build/bin/yume` and `build/bin/yumed` with the pinned, patched
OpenSSL. You can build and run both programs and the optional `libyume` without
BaseFWX. See [the contributor guide](CONTRIBUTING.md) for direct CMake builds.

`ezbuild.sh` installs build dependencies with the system package manager
through `sudo`, such as `sudo apt-get` on Debian and Ubuntu. Set
`YUME_SKIP_DEPS=1` to skip that step. It caches downloads, the patched
OpenSSL build and build locks in `~/.cache/yume`, or in `YUME_CACHE_ROOT`
when that is set.

## Set up a tunnel

`yume-setup` writes a server and client kit: configuration, credentials and a
cover site.

@code bash
sudo cmake --install build
yume-setup init \
  --output ~/yume-kit \
  --host yume.example.com \
  --port 443 \
  --client-name laptop
@end

The [quick start](docs/QUICKSTART.md) continues from here. The kit contains
private keys, so move client files over a trusted channel.

## Programs

@table
| Program | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `yume` | Client: SOCKS5 TCP/UDP listener and Linux managed TUN |
| `yumed` | Server: TLS/HTTP/2 front door, cover site, authentication, policy and exit |
| `yume-setup` | Creates server and client kits, adds and removes clients |
| `yume-doctor` | Checks configuration and credential files |
| `libyume` | Optional C library for embedding the client or server in another program |
| `yume-gui` | Optional desktop window that imports kits and starts, stops and shows clients |
@end

The [desktop GUI](docs/GUI.md) builds with Qt 6 when enabled and talks to each
client only through its control socket. Server [modules](docs/MODULES.md)
receive authorized streams over private UNIX sockets. Chat and file-transfer
libraries are available for future modules.

